From caf4d5dad45a06ce93d0557c0063237bc48b43d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:59:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban ci progress: unreadable runner list + a job
 running on the labels = busy, not offline

When neither `actions/runners` list is readable (403/404: the token lacks admin scope), a self-hosted job queued
past QUEUED_LIMIT_SECONDS was `not_starting`: the card was woken and `runners-offline.<labels>` asserted, even
when the pool was online and working through a queue. Now that case first looks for a job asking for exactly the
same labels that a runner is running or took within QUEUED_LIMIT_SECONDS: this run's own jobs, then the jobs of up
to RECENT_RUNS_MAX (4) in-progress, then recently updated completed, runs of the repo. A hit is `busy` (the
existing non-waking state from 7d78374d70; logged at INFO as "runner list unreadable, but runner X took job N"),
so the park is allowed and nothing is woken; the next sweep re-reads. No hit keeps `not_starting`. Those reads
respect the sweep deadline (OverBudget -> deferred, as the runner-list read does). GitHub-hosted labels read no
run lists.

Scope note: card t_01a1d4f0a0cc8638 asked for the readable-runner-list check too; 7d78374d70 (#2040 follow-ups,
landed 12:48 UTC, 19 min after the incident) already returns `busy` when a readable repo/org list has an online
runner covering the labels. This PR adds the card's remaining item, the 403/404 fallback. The card suggested `ok`
for the fallback; it returns `busy`, main's state for "queued, pool alive".

## Five whys

Problem. 2026-11-13 04:29:12 PST, `kanban_ci_progress.apply` woke t_01a1d49e9453f7fa (owner of state PR #2179,
approved at a7033dde93): "CI run 43423320884 queued 15 min, no runner for labels self-hosted,macOS,hermes-state
... no machine is picking it up", asserted shared blocker `runners-offline.hermes-state`, run outcome
`ci_run_unstartable`. From 04:29:21 the card sat ready with `respawn_guarded: shared_blocker` until the 30-min
probe; an operator `hermes kanban resume` was refused ("resume requires an active_pr hold"). The runner was online:
at 04:36 `gh api repos/example-org/hermes/actions/runners` showed `mini-hermes-state-1 online
busy=true` (labels self-hosted,macOS,ARM64,hermes-state); its _diag logs show back-to-back ~10 min
`hermes_state (macOS)` jobs 03:56-04:36, then #2179's jobs 04:36:30-04:37:29; the run succeeded by 04:42.

Why 1. `ci_run_progress` called any job queued > QUEUED_LIMIT_SECONDS (15 min) with no `runner_name`
`not_starting` (core 2b80c7272d, hermes_cli/kanban_ci_progress.py:150-160).

Why 2. Queue age was its only signal: at 2b80c7272d the module read only the run's jobs (`_fetch_jobs`), no
actions/runners read. (7d78374d70 has since added the runner-list read; a token that cannot list runners still
had only queue age.)

Why 3. The hermes-state pool is one self-hosted runner on daveey-mini running one job at a time; with two or more
core PRs ahead, any macOS job waits >15 min. That is a healthy queue, not an offline pool.

Root (happened). The 11-11 design targeted labels with no runner at all (`self-hosted,ci-fast`) and keyed on queue
time alone, which also matches a busy single-runner pool.

Root (not caught). tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_ci_progress.py covered "queued with no runner" and "started", not
"queued behind a busy online runner" (7d78374d70 added the readable-list case; this adds the unreadable one).

## Proof

RED (new tests on main's module): 2 failed, 48 passed. With runners 403 and a job running on the labels the sweep
woke the card (`['t_...'] == []`); with runners 403 and a job started 11 min ago the 20-min park was refused.
GREEN: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_ci_progress.py 50 passed. A stale (40 min) job, or a recent job on other
labels, still gives `not_starting`; GitHub-hosted labels make no run-list read.
Live read through the broker (user/github), 2026-11-13: repo runner list readable (mini-hermes-state-1 online busy,
labels self-hosted,macOS,ARM64,hermes-state), so main's check already returns `busy` there; org list 403
(admin:org), which matters only to a token without repo admin.
